Title = {MEANet: A Novel Multiscale Edge-Aware Network for Building Change
   Detection in High-Resolution Remote Sensing Images},
Journal = {REMOTE SENSING},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {18},
Number = {2},
Month = {JAN 14},
Abstract = {Highlights What are the main findings? We develop a novel Multiscale
   Edge-Aware Network (MEANet) based on HRNet, which effectively preserves
   high-resolution features and enhances the fusion of spatial and semantic
   information for building change detection. We introduce a multi-scale
   supervised contrastive loss (MSCL) mechanism, which enlarges the feature
   distance between changed and unchanged classes, improving feature
   separability and the discrimination of edge-region pixels prone to
   misclassification. What is the implication of the main finding? By
   explicitly modeling edge-aware representations, MEANet effectively
   reduces false positives and missed detections in high-resolution remote
   sensing change detection tasks. The proposed framework provides a robust
   solution for accurate building change detection, demonstrating strong
   potential for practical urban monitoring and land-use analysis
   applications.Highlights What are the main findings? We develop a novel
   Multiscale Edge-Aware Network (MEANet) based on HRNet, which effectively
   preserves high-resolution features and enhances the fusion of spatial
   and semantic information for building change detection. We introduce a
   multi-scale supervised contrastive loss (MSCL) mechanism, which enlarges
   the feature distance between changed and unchanged classes, improving
   feature separability and the discrimination of edge-region pixels prone
   to misclassification. What is the implication of the main finding? By
   explicitly modeling edge-aware representations, MEANet effectively
   reduces false positives and missed detections in high-resolution remote
   sensing change detection tasks. The proposed framework provides a robust
   solution for accurate building change detection, demonstrating strong
   potential for practical urban monitoring and land-use analysis
   applications.Abstract Remote sensing building change detection (RSBCD)
   is critical for land surface monitoring and understanding interactions
   between human activities and the ecological environment. However,
   existing deep learning-based RSBCD methods often result in mis-detected
   pixels concentrated around object boundaries, mainly due to ambiguous
   object shapes and complex spatial distributions. To address this
   problem, we propose a new Multiscale Edge-Aware change detection Network
   (MEANet) that accurately locates edge pixels of changed objects and
   enhances the separability between changed and unchanged pixels.
   Specifically, a high-resolution feature fusion network is adopted to
   preserve spatial details while integrating deep semantic information,
   and a multi-scale supervised contrastive loss (MSCL) is designed to
   jointly optimize pixel-level discrimination and embedding space
   separability. To further improve the handling of difficult samples, hard
   negative sampling is adopted in the contrastive learning process. We
   conduct comparative experiments on three benchmark datasets. Both Visual
   and quantitative results demonstrate that our new MEANet significantly
   reduces misclassified pixels at object boundaries and achieve superior
   detection accuracy compared to existing methods. Especially on the GZ-CD
   dataset, MEANet improves F1-Score and mIoU by more than 2\% compared
   with ChangeFormer, demonstrating strong robustness in complex scenarios.
   It is worth noting that the performance of MEANet may still be affected
   by extremely complex edge textures or highly blurred boundaries. Future
   work will focus on further improving robustness under such challenges
   and extending the method to broader RSBCD scenarios.},
